#### 2. Trivial `IsNotNull` Assertions Masquerading as Verification (3 tests)

| Test (line) | Problem |
|---|---|
| `GetProduct_Works` (L28) | `Assert.IsNotNull(result)` — confirms *something* came back, never checks it's the right product, right name, or right price |
| `SearchProducts_Works` (L51) | `Assert.IsNotNull(results)` — `List<T>` is never null; never checks the list contains the expected match or excludes non-matches |
| `ExportToCsv_Works` (L81) | `Assert.IsNotNull(csv)` — confirms a non-null string, never verifies the CSV content is correct |

These are functionally equivalent to no assertion. `Search` could return every product or the wrong products and the test would pass.

